<rdf:Description rdf:about="1.2.1.8">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Enzyme"/>
<skos:prefLabel>betaine-aldehyde dehydrogenase</skos:prefLabel>
<skos:altLabel>BADH</skos:altLabel>
<skos:altLabel>betaine aldehyde dehydrogenase</skos:altLabel>
<skos:altLabel>betaine aldehyde oxidase</skos:altLabel>
<activity rdf:resource="1.2.1.8#SIP68A93BF619F5C7DB"/>
<rdfs:comment>In many bacteria, plants and animals, the osmoprotectant betaine is synthesized in two steps: (1) choline to betaine aldehyde and (2) betaine aldehyde to betaine.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>This enzyme is involved in the second step and appears to be the same in plants, animals and bacteria.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>In contrast, different enzymes are involved in the first reaction.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>In plants, this reaction is catalyzed by EC 1.14.15.7, whereas in animals and many bacteria, it is catalyzed by either membrane-bound EC 1.1.99.1 or soluble EC 1.1.3.17.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>In some bacteria, betaine is synthesized from glycine through the actions of EC 2.1.1.156 and EC 2.1.1.157.</rdfs:comment>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/9792097"/>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/12466265"/>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/12604197"/>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/12736784"/>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/13192104"/>
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="1.2.1.-"/>
<skos:broaderTransitive rdf:resource="1.2.1.-"/>
</rdf:Description>
