<rdf:Description rdf:about="1.2.1.73">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Enzyme"/>
<skos:prefLabel>sulfoacetaldehyde dehydrogenase</skos:prefLabel>
<activity rdf:resource="1.2.1.73#SIP3BF1C5EE0833BC95"/>
<rdfs:comment>Part of a bacterial pathway that can utilize the amino group of taurine as a sole source of nitrogen for growth.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>At physiological concentrations, NAD(+) cannot be replaced by NADP(+).</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>Specific for sulfoacetaldehyde, as formaldehyde, acetaldehyde, betaine aldehyde, propanal, glyceraldehyde, phosphonoaldehyde, glyoxylate, glycolaldehyde and 2-oxobutyrate are not substrates.</rdfs:comment>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/18506422"/>
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="1.2.1.-"/>
<skos:broaderTransitive rdf:resource="1.2.1.-"/>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="1.2.1.73#SIP3BF1C5EE0833BC95">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Catalytic_Activity"/>
<rdfs:label>H2O + NAD(+) + sulfoacetaldehyde = 2 H(+) + NADH + sulfoacetate.</rdfs:label>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/18506422">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Citation"/>
<skos:exactMatch rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/pubmed/18506422"/>
<foaf:primaryTopicOf rdf:resource="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/18506422"/>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="1.2.1.74">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Enzyme"/>
<skos:prefLabel>abieta-7,13-dien-18-al dehydrogenase</skos:prefLabel>
<skos:altLabel>abietadienal dehydrogenase</skos:altLabel>
<activity rdf:resource="1.2.1.74#SIP426961C1E95DFF3E"/>
<rdfs:comment>Abietic acid is the principle component of conifer resin.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>This enzyme catalyzes the last step of the pathway of abietic acid biosynthesis in Abies grandis (grand fir).</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>The activity has been demonstrated in cell-free stem extracts of A. grandis, was present in the cytoplasm, and required NAD(+) as cofactor.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>The enzyme is expressed constitutively at a high level, and is not inducible by wounding of the plant tissue.</rdfs:comment>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/8311462"/>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/12232380"/>
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="1.2.1.-"/>
<skos:broaderTransitive rdf:resource="1.2.1.-"/>
</rdf:Description>
