<rdf:Description rdf:about="1.3.5.2">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Enzyme"/>
<replaces rdf:resource="1.3.99.11"/>
<skos:prefLabel>dihydroorotate dehydrogenase (quinone)</skos:prefLabel>
<skos:altLabel>DHOD</skos:altLabel>
<skos:altLabel>DHOdehase</skos:altLabel>
<skos:altLabel>DHODH</skos:altLabel>
<activity rdf:resource="1.3.5.2#SIPD17A66EAAF5B3B17"/>
<activity rdf:resource="1.3.5.2#SIPC2BF440ED6691D6A"/>
<activity rdf:resource="1.3.5.2#SIP0C303E1A8770AB86"/>
<rdfs:comment>This Class 2 dihydroorotate dehydrogenase enzyme contains FMN.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>The enzyme is found in eukaryotes in the mitochondrial membrane, in cyanobacteria, and in some Gram-negative and Gram-positive bacteria associated with the cytoplasmic membrane.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>The reaction is the only redox reaction in the de-novo biosynthesis of pyrimidine nucleotides.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>The best quinone electron acceptors for the enzyme from bovine liver are ubiquinone-6 and ubiquinone-7, although simple quinones, such as benzoquinone, can also act as acceptor at lower rates.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>Methyl-, ethyl-, tert-butyl and benzyl-(S)-dihydroorotates are also substrates, but methyl esters of (S)-1-methyl and (S)-3-methyl and (S)-1,3-dimethyldihydroorotates are not.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>Class 1 dihydroorotate dehydrogenases use either fumarate (EC 1.3.98.1), NAD(+) (EC 1.3.1.14) or NADP(+) (EC 1.3.1.15) as electron acceptor.</rdfs:comment>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/216313"/>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/3733756"/>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/9693067"/>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/10074342"/>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/17154530"/>
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="1.3.5.-"/>
<skos:broaderTransitive rdf:resource="1.3.5.-"/>
</rdf:Description>
