<rdf:Description rdf:about="1.4.1.21">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Enzyme"/>
<skos:prefLabel>aspartate dehydrogenase</skos:prefLabel>
<skos:altLabel>NAD-dependent aspartate dehydrogenase</skos:altLabel>
<skos:altLabel>NADH2-dependent aspartate dehydrogenase</skos:altLabel>
<skos:altLabel>NADP(+)-dependent aspartate dehydrogenase</skos:altLabel>
<activity rdf:resource="1.4.1.21#SIP080E68F182F0590B"/>
<activity rdf:resource="1.4.1.21#SIP770875BD86742830"/>
<rdfs:comment>The enzyme is strictly specific for L-aspartate as substrate.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>It produces the unstable compound 2-iminosuccinate, which, in the presence of water, hydrolyzes spontaneously to form oxaloacetate.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>The enzyme from some archaea and thermophilic bacteria is likely to transfer 2-iminosuccinate directly to EC 2.5.1.72 preventing its hydrolysis and enabling the de novo biosynthesis of NAD(+).</rdfs:comment>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/9819709"/>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/12496312"/>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/16731057"/>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/17651440"/>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/21468714"/>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/21821928"/>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/22120624"/>
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="1.4.1.-"/>
<skos:broaderTransitive rdf:resource="1.4.1.-"/>
</rdf:Description>
