<replaces rdf:resource="1.6.6.5"/>
<replaces rdf:resource="1.7.99.3"/>
<replaces rdf:resource="1.9.3.2"/>
<skos:prefLabel>nitrite reductase (NO-forming)</skos:prefLabel>
<skos:altLabel>cd-cytochrome nitrite reductase</skos:altLabel>
<skos:altLabel>cytochrome c-551:O2, NO2(+) oxidoreductase</skos:altLabel>
<skos:altLabel>cytochrome cd</skos:altLabel>
<skos:altLabel>cytochrome cd1</skos:altLabel>
<skos:altLabel>hydroxylamine (acceptor) reductase</skos:altLabel>
<skos:altLabel>methyl viologen-nitrite reductase</skos:altLabel>
<skos:altLabel>nitrite reductase</skos:altLabel>
<skos:altLabel>nitrite reductase (cytochrome; NO-forming)</skos:altLabel>
<skos:altLabel>nitrite reductase (cytochrome)</skos:altLabel>
<skos:altLabel>Pseudomonas cytochrome oxidase</skos:altLabel>
<activity rdf:resource="1.7.2.1#SIP0A6A69D7D216F54C"/>
<rdfs:comment>The reaction is catalyzed by two types of enzymes, found in the perimplasm of denitrifying bacteria.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>One type comprises proteins containing multiple copper centers, the other a heme protein, cytochrome cd1.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>Acceptors include c-type cytochromes such as cytochrome c-550 or cytochrome c-551 from Paracoccus denitrificans or Pseudomonas aeruginosa, and small blue copper proteins such as azurin and pseudoazurin.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>Cytochrome cd1 also has oxidase and hydroxylamine reductase activities.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>May also catalyze the reaction of EC 1.7.99.1 since this is a well-known activity of cytochrome cd1.</rdfs:comment>
