<skos:altLabel>FMO</skos:altLabel>
<skos:altLabel>methylphenyltetrahydropyridine N-monooxygenase</skos:altLabel>
<skos:altLabel>mixed-function amine oxidase</skos:altLabel>
<skos:altLabel>N,N-dimethylaniline monooxygenase</skos:altLabel>
<skos:altLabel>Ziegler's enzyme</skos:altLabel>
<activity rdf:resource="1.14.13.8#SIP84256A9B7D6FC436"/>
<activity rdf:resource="1.14.13.8#SIP4CFF0C84EB20A93F"/>
<activity rdf:resource="1.14.13.8#SIP5E4CA038523926A8"/>
<rdfs:comment>A broad spectrum monooxygenase that accepts substrates as diverse as hydrazines, phosphines, boron-containing compounds, sulfides, selenides, iodide, as well as primary, secondary and tertiary amines.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>Is distinct from other monooxygenases in that the enzyme forms a relatively stable hydroperoxy flavin intermediate.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>Generally converts nucleophilic heteroatom-containing chemicals and drugs into harmless, readily excreted metabolites.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>For example, N-oxygenation is largely responsible for the detoxification of the dopaminergic neurotoxin 1-methyl-4-phenyl-1,2,3,6-tetrahydropyridine (MPTP).</rdfs:comment>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/3262153"/>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/3949735"/>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/4381353"/>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/7672012"/>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/16402899"/>
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="1.14.13.-"/>
<skos:broaderTransitive rdf:resource="1.14.13.-"/>
</rdf:Description>
