<rdf:Description rdf:about="1.14.13.92">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Enzyme"/>
<skos:prefLabel>phenylacetone monooxygenase</skos:prefLabel>
<skos:altLabel>PAMO</skos:altLabel>
<activity rdf:resource="1.14.13.92#SIP05111CE6D886A786"/>
<rdfs:comment>NADH cannot replace NADPH as cosubstrate.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>In addition to phenylacetone, which is the best substrate found to date, this Baeyer-Villiger monooxygenase can oxidize other aromatic ketones [1-(4-hydroxyphenyl)propan-2-one, 1-(4-hydroxyphenyl)propan-2-one and 3-phenylbutan-2-one], some alipatic ketones (e.g. dodecan-2-one) and sulfides (e.g. 1-methyl-4-(methylsulfanyl)benzene).</rdfs:comment>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/15328411"/>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/15599520"/>
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="1.14.13.-"/>
<skos:broaderTransitive rdf:resource="1.14.13.-"/>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="1.14.13.92#SIP05111CE6D886A786">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Catalytic_Activity"/>
<rdfs:label>H(+) + NADPH + O2 + phenylacetone = benzyl acetate + H2O + NADP(+).</rdfs:label>
</rdf:Description>
