<rdf:Description rdf:about="1.14.13.107">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Enzyme"/>
<skos:prefLabel>limonene 1,2-monooxygenase</skos:prefLabel>
<activity rdf:resource="1.14.13.107#SIPF77BC18870960257"/>
<activity rdf:resource="1.14.13.107#SIP63609AA133C2EFDA"/>
<activity rdf:resource="1.14.13.107#SIP3F0413D51D9E61B0"/>
<activity rdf:resource="1.14.13.107#SIPB0CEFD1AFE203154"/>
<rdfs:comment>Limonene is the most widespread terpene and is formed by more than 300 plants.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>Rhodococcus erythropolis DCL14, a Gram-positive bacterium, is able to grow on both (S)-limonene and (R)-limonene as the sole source of carbon and energy.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>NADPH can act instead of NADH, although more slowly.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>It has not been established if the product formed is optically pure or a mixture of two enantiomers.</rdfs:comment>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/10224006"/>
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="1.14.13.-"/>
<skos:broaderTransitive rdf:resource="1.14.13.-"/>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="1.14.13.107#SIPF77BC18870960257">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Catalytic_Activity"/>
<rdfs:label>(4S)-limonene + H(+) + NADPH + O2 = H2O + limonene 1,2-epoxide + NADP(+).</rdfs:label>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="1.14.13.107#SIP63609AA133C2EFDA">
