<replaces rdf:resource="1.14.13.n4"/>
<skos:prefLabel>vitamin D3 24-hydroxylase</skos:prefLabel>
<skos:altLabel>CYP24A1</skos:altLabel>
<activity rdf:resource="1.14.15.16#SIPC06188A00F58042C"/>
<activity rdf:resource="1.14.15.16#SIPC14CEE5FD37779EB"/>
<activity rdf:resource="1.14.15.16#SIPE3B72BBB1ACC46A2"/>
<activity rdf:resource="1.14.15.16#SIP4A531D06BFCF533B"/>
<activity rdf:resource="1.14.15.16#SIP40AE1C35F815D308"/>
<activity rdf:resource="1.14.15.16#SIPB04C43CC91CD1DF8"/>
<rdfs:comment>The enzyme can perform up to 6 rounds of hydroxylation of the substrate calcitriol leading to calcitroic acid.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>The human enzyme also shows 23-hydroxylating activity leading to 1,25 dihydroxyvitamin D3-26,23-lactone as end product while the mouse and rat enzymes do not.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>The initial source of the electrons is NADPH, which transfers the electrons to the adrenodoxin via EC 1.18.1.6.</rdfs:comment>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/15078099"/>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/15358094"/>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/15544953"/>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/15574355"/>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/16516540"/>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/16617161"/>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/17646648"/>
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="1.14.15.-"/>
<skos:broaderTransitive rdf:resource="1.14.15.-"/>
</rdf:Description>
