</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="1.14.99.54">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Enzyme"/>
<skos:prefLabel>lytic cellulose monooxygenase (C1-hydroxylating)</skos:prefLabel>
<skos:altLabel>LPMO</skos:altLabel>
<skos:altLabel>lytic polysaccharide monooxygenase</skos:altLabel>
<activity rdf:resource="1.14.99.54#SIP538525FE7885C74D"/>
<rdfs:comment>This enzyme, found in fungi and bacteria, cleaves cellulose in an oxidative manner.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>The cellulose fragments that are formed contain a D-glucono-1,5-lactone residue at the reducing end, which hydrolyzes quickly and spontaneously to the aldonic acid.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>The electrons are provided in vivo by the cytochrome b domain of EC 1.1.99.18.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>Ascorbate can serve as the electron donor in vitro.</rdfs:comment>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/22004347"/>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/22188218"/>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/22578542"/>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/23124232"/>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/26185526"/>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/27152023"/>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/27213015"/>
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="1.14.99.-"/>
<skos:broaderTransitive rdf:resource="1.14.99.-"/>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="1.14.99.54#SIP538525FE7885C74D">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Catalytic_Activity"/>
<rdfs:label>[(1->4)-beta-D-glucosyl]n+m + reduced acceptor + O2 = [(1->4)-beta-D-glucosyl]m-1-(1->4)-D-glucono-1,5-lactone + [(1->4)-beta-D-glucosyl]n + acceptor + H2O.</rdfs:label>
</rdf:Description>
