<rdf:Description rdf:about="1.17.1.4">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Enzyme"/>
<replaces rdf:resource="1.1.1.204"/>
<replaces rdf:resource="1.2.1.37"/>
<skos:prefLabel>xanthine dehydrogenase</skos:prefLabel>
<skos:altLabel>NAD-xanthine dehydrogenase</skos:altLabel>
<skos:altLabel>xanthine/NAD(+) oxidoreductase</skos:altLabel>
<skos:altLabel>xanthine-NAD oxidoreductase</skos:altLabel>
<skos:altLabel>xanthine oxidoreductase</skos:altLabel>
<activity rdf:resource="1.17.1.4#SIPAD0D6371EBC452F7"/>
<activity rdf:resource="1.17.1.4#SIP942305E64D39FFEE"/>
<rdfs:comment>Acts on a variety of purines and aldehydes, including hypoxanthine.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>The mammalian enzyme can also convert all-trans retinol to all-trans-retinoate, while the substrate is bound to a retinoid-binding protein.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>The enzyme from eukaryotes contains [2Fe-2S], FAD and a molybdenum center.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>The mammallian enzyme predominantly exists as the NAD-dependent dehydrogenase (EC 1.17.1.4).</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>During purification the enzyme is largely converted to an O2-dependent form, EC 1.17.3.2.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>The conversion can be triggered by several mechanisms, including the oxidation of cysteine thiols to form disulfide bonds (which can be catalyzed by EC 1.8.4.7 in the presence of glutathione disulfide) or limited proteolysis, which results in irreversible conversion.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>The conversion can also occur in vivo.</rdfs:comment>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/2610112"/>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/3294898"/>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/3459393"/>
