<rdf:Description rdf:about="2.3.1.304">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Enzyme"/>
<skos:prefLabel>poly[(R)-3-hydroxyalkanoate] polymerase</skos:prefLabel>
<skos:altLabel>PHA synthase</skos:altLabel>
<activity rdf:resource="2.3.1.304#SIP0BBBAF5AD8211D73"/>
<activity rdf:resource="2.3.1.304#SIPF895555B9BD16696"/>
<rdfs:comment>This is the key enzyme in the biosynthesis of polyhydroxyalkanoates (PHA), linear polyesters produced by bacteria as a means of carbon and energy storage.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>The enzyme catalyzes the stereoselective, covalent linkage of (3R)-3-hydroxyacyl-CoA thioesters in a transesterification reaction with concomitant release of coenzyme A.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>The growing polymer is attached to a conserved active site L-cysteine residue.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>Three types of PHA synthases have been proposed based on their substrate specificity and enzyme structure.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>Type I and type III synthases preferentially polymerize short chain hydroxyalkanoate monomers containing 3-5 carbon atoms.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>The difference between these two types is that type I synthases are composed of only a single subunit (PhaC), whereas type III synthases are composed of two different subunits, PhaC and PhaE. Type II synthases are also composed of a single subunit (PhaC), but preferentially polymerize monomers containing more than 5 carbon atoms.</rdfs:comment>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/2078525"/>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/7957260"/>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/9888824"/>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/10880359"/>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/11170423"/>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/28884324"/>
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="2.3.1.-"/>
<skos:broaderTransitive rdf:resource="2.3.1.-"/>
</rdf:Description>
