<rdf:Description rdf:about="2.4.1.159">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Enzyme"/>
<skos:prefLabel>flavonol-3-O-glucoside L-rhamnosyltransferase</skos:prefLabel>
<skos:altLabel>uridine diphosphorhamnose-flavonol 3-O-glucoside rhamnosyltransferase</skos:altLabel>
<activity rdf:resource="2.4.1.159#SIP426741A4396F2410"/>
<rdfs:comment>A configuration-inverting rhamnosyltransferase that converts flavonol 3-O-glucosides to 3-O-rutinosides.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>Also acts, more slowly, on rutin, quercetin 3-O-galactoside and flavonol 3-O-rhamnosides.</rdfs:comment>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/12900416"/>
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="2.4.1.-"/>
<skos:broaderTransitive rdf:resource="2.4.1.-"/>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="2.4.1.159#SIP426741A4396F2410">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Catalytic_Activity"/>
<rdfs:label>a flavonol 3-O-beta-D-glucoside + UDP-beta-L-rhamnose = a flavonol 3-O-[alpha-L-rhamnosyl-(1->6)-beta-D-glucoside] + H(+) + UDP.</rdfs:label>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/12900416">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Citation"/>
<skos:exactMatch rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/pubmed/12900416"/>
<foaf:primaryTopicOf rdf:resource="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/12900416"/>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="2.4.1.160">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Enzyme"/>
<skos:prefLabel>pyridoxine 5'-O-beta-D-glucosyltransferase</skos:prefLabel>
<skos:altLabel>UDP-glucose-pyridoxine glucosyltransferase</skos:altLabel>
<activity rdf:resource="2.4.1.160#SIP77E8CBBC9112C3ED"/>
<rdfs:comment>4'-deoxypyridoxine and pyridoxamine can also act as acceptors, more slowly.</rdfs:comment>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/6217302"/>
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="2.4.1.-"/>
<skos:broaderTransitive rdf:resource="2.4.1.-"/>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="2.4.1.160#SIP77E8CBBC9112C3ED">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Catalytic_Activity"/>
<rdfs:label>pyridoxine + UDP-alpha-D-glucose = 5'-O-beta-D-glucosylpyridoxine + H(+) + UDP.</rdfs:label>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/6217302">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Citation"/>
<skos:exactMatch rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/pubmed/6217302"/>
<foaf:primaryTopicOf rdf:resource="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/6217302"/>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="2.4.1.161">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Enzyme"/>
<skos:prefLabel>oligosaccharide 4-alpha-D-glucosyltransferase</skos:prefLabel>
<skos:altLabel>alpha-1,4-transglucosylase</skos:altLabel>
<skos:altLabel>amylase III</skos:altLabel>
<activity rdf:resource="2.4.1.161#SIPEF6381AE247D927F"/>
<rdfs:comment>The enzyme acts on amylose, amylopectin, glycogen and maltooligosaccharides.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>No detectable free glucose is formed, indicating the enzyme does not act as a hydrolase.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>The enzyme from the bacterium Cellvibrio japonicus has the highest activity with maltotriose as a donor, and also accepts maltose, while the enzyme from amoeba does not accept maltose.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>Oligosaccharides with 1->6 linkages cannot function as donors, but can act as acceptors.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>Unlike EC 2.4.1.25, this enzyme can transfer only a single glucosyl residue.</rdfs:comment>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/2423097"/>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/2423098"/>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/23132856"/>
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="2.4.1.-"/>
<skos:broaderTransitive rdf:resource="2.4.1.-"/>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="2.4.1.161#SIPEF6381AE247D927F">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Catalytic_Activity"/>
<rdfs:label>Transfers the non-reducing terminal alpha-D-glucose residue from a (1->4)-alpha-D-glucan to the 4-position of a free glucose or of a glucosyl residue at the non-reducing terminus of a (1->4)-alpha-D-glucan, thus bringing about the rearrangement of oligosaccharides.</rdfs:label>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/2423097">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Citation"/>
<skos:exactMatch rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/pubmed/2423097"/>
<foaf:primaryTopicOf rdf:resource="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/2423097"/>
</rdf:Description>
