<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/24828502">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Citation"/>
<skos:exactMatch rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/pubmed/24828502"/>
<foaf:primaryTopicOf rdf:resource="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24828502"/>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="2.4.1.333">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Enzyme"/>
<skos:prefLabel>1,2-beta-oligoglucan phosphorylase</skos:prefLabel>
<activity rdf:resource="2.4.1.333#SIP8219E4430835FA71"/>
<rdfs:comment>The enzyme has been isolated from the bacterium Listeria innocua.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>It catalyzes the reversible phosphorolysis of beta-(1->2)-D-glucans.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>The minimum length of the substrate for the phosphorolytic reaction is 3 D-glucose units.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>In the synthetic reaction starting from sophorose and alpha-D-glucose 1-phosphate the average polymerisation degree is 39.</rdfs:comment>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/24647662"/>
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="2.4.1.-"/>
<skos:broaderTransitive rdf:resource="2.4.1.-"/>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="2.4.1.333#SIP8219E4430835FA71">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Catalytic_Activity"/>
<rdfs:label>[(1->2)-beta-D-glucosyl](n) + phosphate = [(1->2)-beta-D-glucosyl](n-1) + alpha-D-glucose 1-phosphate.</rdfs:label>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/24647662">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Citation"/>
<skos:exactMatch rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/pubmed/24647662"/>
<foaf:primaryTopicOf rdf:resource="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24647662"/>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="2.4.1.334">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Enzyme"/>
<skos:prefLabel>1,3-alpha-oligoglucan phosphorylase</skos:prefLabel>
<activity rdf:resource="2.4.1.334#SIP317B3252B44151F9"/>
<rdfs:comment>The enzyme, isolated from the bacterium Clostridium phytofermentans, catalyzes a reversible reaction.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>Substrates for the phosphorolytic reaction are alpha-1,3-linked oligoglucans with a polymerisation degree of 3 or more.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>Nigerose (i.e. 3-O-alpha-D-glucopyranosyl-D-glucopyranose) is not phosphorylyzed but can serve as substrate in the reverse direction (cf. EC 2.4.1.279).</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="2.4.1.-"/>
<skos:broaderTransitive rdf:resource="2.4.1.-"/>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="2.4.1.334#SIP317B3252B44151F9">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Catalytic_Activity"/>
<rdfs:label>[(1->3)-alpha-D-glucosyl](n) + phosphate = [(1->3)-alpha-D-glucosyl](n-1) + beta-D-glucose 1-phosphate.</rdfs:label>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="2.4.1.335">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Enzyme"/>
<skos:prefLabel>dolichyl N-acetyl-alpha-D-glucosaminyl phosphate 3-beta-D-2,3-diacetamido-2,3-dideoxy-beta-D-glucuronosyltransferase</skos:prefLabel>
<activity rdf:resource="2.4.1.335#SIPAF511A89C1C1DAF5"/>
<rdfs:comment>The enzyme, characterized from the methanogenic archaeon Methanococcus voltae, participates in the N-glycosylation of proteins.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>Dolichol used by archaea is different from that used by eukaryotes; it is much shorter (C55-C60), it is alpha,omega-saturated and it may have additional unsaturated positions in the chain.</rdfs:comment>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/23624439"/>
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="2.4.1.-"/>
<skos:broaderTransitive rdf:resource="2.4.1.-"/>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="2.4.1.335#SIPAF511A89C1C1DAF5">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Catalytic_Activity"/>
<rdfs:label>an archaeal dolichyl N-acetyl-alpha-D-glucosaminyl phosphate + UDP-2,3-diacetamido-2,3-dideoxy-alpha-D-glucuronate = an archaeal dolichyl 3-O-(2,3-diacetamido-2,3-dideoxy- beta-D-glucuronosyl)-N-acetyl- alpha-D-glucosaminyl phosphate + H(+) + UDP.</rdfs:label>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/23624439">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Citation"/>
<skos:exactMatch rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/pubmed/23624439"/>
<foaf:primaryTopicOf rdf:resource="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23624439"/>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="2.4.1.336">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Enzyme"/>
<replaces rdf:resource="2.4.1.157"/>
<skos:prefLabel>monoglucosyldiacylglycerol synthase</skos:prefLabel>
<activity rdf:resource="2.4.1.336#SIPACE1ABA4FC0AA701"/>
<rdfs:comment>The enzymes from cyanobacteria are involved in the biosynthesis of galactolipids found in their photosynthetic membranes.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>The enzyme belongs to the GT2 family of configuration-inverting glycosyltranferases. cf. EC 2.4.1.337.</rdfs:comment>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/16714404"/>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/24370445"/>
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="2.4.1.-"/>
<skos:broaderTransitive rdf:resource="2.4.1.-"/>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="2.4.1.336#SIPACE1ABA4FC0AA701">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Catalytic_Activity"/>
<rdfs:label>a 1,2-diacyl-sn-glycerol + UDP-alpha-D-glucose = a 1,2-diacyl-3-O-(beta-D-glucopyranosyl)-sn-glycerol + H(+) + UDP.</rdfs:label>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/16714404">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Citation"/>
<skos:exactMatch rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/pubmed/16714404"/>
<foaf:primaryTopicOf rdf:resource="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/16714404"/>
<dcterms:identifier>doi:10.1104/pp.106.082859</dcterms:identifier>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/24370445">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Citation"/>
<skos:exactMatch rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/pubmed/24370445"/>
<foaf:primaryTopicOf rdf:resource="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24370445"/>
<dcterms:identifier>doi:10.1016/j.bbalip.2013.12.007</dcterms:identifier>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="2.4.1.337">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Enzyme"/>
<replaces rdf:resource="2.4.1.157"/>
<skos:prefLabel>1,2-diacylglycerol 3-alpha-glucosyltransferase</skos:prefLabel>
<skos:altLabel>1,2-diacylglycerol 3-glucosyltransferase</skos:altLabel>
<skos:altLabel>UDP-glucose-diacylglycerol glucosyltransferase</skos:altLabel>
<skos:altLabel>uridine diphosphoglucose-diacylglycerol glucosyltransferase</skos:altLabel>
<activity rdf:resource="2.4.1.337#SIPD928ADE29BEAE560"/>
<rdfs:comment>The enzyme from the bacterium Acholeplasma laidlawii, which lacks a celll wall, produces the major non-bilayer lipid in the organism.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>The enzyme from the bacterium Agrobacterium tumefaciens acts under phosphate deprivation, generating glycolipids as surrogates for phospholipids.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>The enzyme belongs to the GT4 family of configuration-retaining glycosyltransferases.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>Many diacylglycerols with long-chain acyl groups can act as acceptors. cf. EC 2.4.1.336.</rdfs:comment>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/8995384"/>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/11294844"/>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/12911309"/>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/24558041"/>
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="2.4.1.-"/>
<skos:broaderTransitive rdf:resource="2.4.1.-"/>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="2.4.1.337#SIPD928ADE29BEAE560">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Catalytic_Activity"/>
<rdfs:label>a 1,2-diacyl-sn-glycerol + UDP-alpha-D-glucose = a 1,2-diacyl-3-O-(alpha-D-glucopyranosyl)-sn-glycerol + H(+) + UDP.</rdfs:label>
</rdf:Description>
