<rdf:Description rdf:about="2.4.1.388">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Enzyme"/>
<skos:prefLabel>glucosylgalactose phosphorylase</skos:prefLabel>
<skos:altLabel>4-O-beta-D-glucosyl-D-galactose phosphorylase</skos:altLabel>
<activity rdf:resource="2.4.1.388#SIP568F9EB3E5275833"/>
<rdfs:comment>The enzyme from the bacterium Paenibacillus polymyxa belongs to glycoside hydrolase family 94.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>It has a much lower activity with 4-O-beta-D-glucosyl-L-arabinose.</rdfs:comment>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/34541742"/>
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="2.4.1.-"/>
<skos:broaderTransitive rdf:resource="2.4.1.-"/>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="2.4.1.388#SIP568F9EB3E5275833">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Catalytic_Activity"/>
<rdfs:label>beta-D-glucosyl-(1->4)-D-galactose + phosphate = alpha-D-glucose 1-phosphate + D-galactose.</rdfs:label>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/34541742">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Citation"/>
<skos:exactMatch rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/pubmed/34541742"/>
<foaf:primaryTopicOf rdf:resource="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34541742"/>
<dcterms:identifier>doi:10.1002/cbic.202100401</dcterms:identifier>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="2.4.1.389">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Enzyme"/>
<skos:prefLabel>solabiose phosphorylase</skos:prefLabel>
<activity rdf:resource="2.4.1.389#SIPD8D3C738CB0262C8"/>
<rdfs:comment>The enzyme, characterized from the bacterium Paenibacillus borealis, belongs to glycoside hydrolase family 94 (GH94).</rdfs:comment>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/34997180"/>
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="2.4.1.-"/>
<skos:broaderTransitive rdf:resource="2.4.1.-"/>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="2.4.1.389#SIPD8D3C738CB0262C8">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Catalytic_Activity"/>
<rdfs:label>phosphate + solabiose = alpha-D-glucose 1-phosphate + D-galactose.</rdfs:label>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/34997180">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Citation"/>
<skos:exactMatch rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/pubmed/34997180"/>
<foaf:primaryTopicOf rdf:resource="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34997180"/>
<dcterms:identifier>doi:10.1038/s41598-021-04421-2</dcterms:identifier>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="2.4.1.390">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Enzyme"/>
<skos:prefLabel>4,3-alpha-glucanotransferase</skos:prefLabel>
<activity rdf:resource="2.4.1.390#SIP7A77AC16E7849B34"/>
<rdfs:comment>The enzyme, characterized from the bacterium Lactobacillus fermentum NCC 2970, possesses hydrolysis and transglycosylase activities on malto-oligosaccharides with a degree of polymerization of at least 6, as well as polymers such as amylose, potato starch, and amylopectin.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>The enzyme, which belongs to glycoside hydrolase 70 (GH70) family, attaches the glucosyl residues by alpha(1->3) linkages in both linear and branched orientations.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>While capable of forming large polymers, the enzyme produces mainly oligosaccharides in vitro.</rdfs:comment>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/28059108"/>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/34708648"/>
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="2.4.1.-"/>
<skos:broaderTransitive rdf:resource="2.4.1.-"/>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="2.4.1.390#SIP7A77AC16E7849B34">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Catalytic_Activity"/>
<rdfs:label>formation of a mixed (1->4)/(1->3)-alpha-D-glucan from (1->4)-alpha-D-glucans.</rdfs:label>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/28059108">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Citation"/>
<skos:exactMatch rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/pubmed/28059108"/>
<foaf:primaryTopicOf rdf:resource="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/28059108"/>
<dcterms:identifier>doi:10.1038/srep39761</dcterms:identifier>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/34708648">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Citation"/>
<skos:exactMatch rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/pubmed/34708648"/>
<foaf:primaryTopicOf rdf:resource="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34708648"/>
<dcterms:identifier>doi:10.1021/acs.jafc.1c05657</dcterms:identifier>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="2.4.1.391">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Enzyme"/>
<skos:prefLabel>beta-1,2-glucosyltransferase</skos:prefLabel>
<activity rdf:resource="2.4.1.391#SIP6C48052B5C1648F7"/>
<rdfs:comment>The enzyme, characterized from the bacterium Ignavibacterium album, transfers a glucosyl residue from the non-reducing end of a 1,2-beta-D-glucan to a glucose residue of an acceptor molecule, forming a beta(1,2) linkage.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>The donor molecule can be as small as sophorose (which contains two glucosyl residues).</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>The enzyme has a very broad specificity for the acceptor, and can act on various aryl- and alkyl-glucosides.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>In addition, the accepting glucose unit can be in either alpha or beta configuration.</rdfs:comment>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/35065074"/>
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="2.4.1.-"/>
<skos:broaderTransitive rdf:resource="2.4.1.-"/>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="2.4.1.391#SIP6C48052B5C1648F7">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Catalytic_Activity"/>
<rdfs:label>[(1->2)-beta-D-glucosyl]n + a D-glucoside = [(1->2)-beta-D-glucosyl]n-1 + a beta-D-glucosyl-(1->2)-D-glucoside.</rdfs:label>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/35065074">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Citation"/>
<skos:exactMatch rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/pubmed/35065074"/>
<foaf:primaryTopicOf rdf:resource="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35065074"/>
<dcterms:identifier>doi:10.1016/j.jbc.2022.101606</dcterms:identifier>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="2.4.1.392">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Enzyme"/>
<skos:prefLabel>3-O-beta-D-glucopyranosyl-beta-D-glucuronide phosphorylase</skos:prefLabel>
<activity rdf:resource="2.4.1.392#SIP80FE765446DF085B"/>
<rdfs:comment>The enzyme, characterized from the bacterium Paenibacillus borealis, catalyzes a reversible reaction, transferring a glucosyl residue attached by a beta(1,3) linkage to a D-glucuronate residue (either free or as a part of a beta-D-glucuronide) to a free phosphate, generating alpha-D-glucopyranose 1-phosphate.</rdfs:comment>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/35947916"/>
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="2.4.1.-"/>
<skos:broaderTransitive rdf:resource="2.4.1.-"/>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="2.4.1.392#SIP80FE765446DF085B">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Catalytic_Activity"/>
<rdfs:label>a 3-O-beta-D-glucosyl-beta-D-glucuronoside + phosphate = a beta-D-glucuronoside + alpha-D-glucopyranose 1-phosphate.</rdfs:label>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/35947916">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Citation"/>
<skos:exactMatch rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/pubmed/35947916"/>
<foaf:primaryTopicOf rdf:resource="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35947916"/>
<dcterms:identifier>doi:10.1016/j.bbrc.2022.07.098</dcterms:identifier>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="2.4.1.393">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Enzyme"/>
<skos:prefLabel>MMP alpha-(1->4)-mannosyltransferase</skos:prefLabel>
<activity rdf:resource="2.4.1.393#SIPC6E6F0BF25BFE03D"/>
<activity rdf:resource="2.4.1.393#SIPC9BF58D9C43FDFF6"/>
<rdfs:comment>The enzyme, present in mycobacterial species that produce a 3-O-methylmannose polysaccharide (MMP), is involved in recycling and biosynthesis of the polymer.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>The enzyme has the highest activity with 3-O-methylated mannosides with 4-6 residues.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>The residue at the reducing end of the substrate is often dimethylated, with the second methyl group attached at the O-1 position.</rdfs:comment>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/36707645"/>
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="2.4.1.-"/>
<skos:broaderTransitive rdf:resource="2.4.1.-"/>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="2.4.1.393#SIPC6E6F0BF25BFE03D">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Catalytic_Activity"/>
<rdfs:label>[3-O-methyl-alpha-D-mannosyl-(1->4)](n)-3-O-methyl-D-mannose + GDP-alpha-D-mannose = alpha-D-mannosyl-(1->4)-[3-O-methyl-alpha-D-mannosyl-(1->4)](n)-3-O-methyl-D-mannose + GDP + H(+).</rdfs:label>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="2.4.1.393#SIPC9BF58D9C43FDFF6">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Catalytic_Activity"/>
<rdfs:label>[3-O-methyl-alpha-D-mannosyl-(1->4)](n)-1-O,3-O-dimethyl-alpha-D-mannose + GDP-alpha-D-mannose = alpha-D-mannosyl-(1->4)-[3-O-methyl-alpha-D-mannosyl-(1->4)](n)-1-O,3-O-dimethyl-alpha-D-mannose + GDP + H(+).</rdfs:label>
</rdf:Description>
