<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Enzyme"/>
<skos:prefLabel>oligo-1,6-glucosidase</skos:prefLabel>
<skos:altLabel>alpha-methylglucosidase</skos:altLabel>
<skos:altLabel>isomaltase</skos:altLabel>
<skos:altLabel>oligosaccharide alpha-1,6-glucosidase</skos:altLabel>
<skos:altLabel>sucrase-isomaltase</skos:altLabel>
<activity rdf:resource="3.2.1.10#SIP2FF83AB4E2A21F76"/>
<rdfs:comment>This enzyme, like EC 3.2.1.33, can release an alpha-1->6-linked glucose, whereas the shortest chain that can be released by EC 3.2.1.41, EC 3.2.1.142 and EC 3.2.1.68 is maltose.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>It also hydrolyzes isomaltulose (palatinose), isomaltotriose and panose, but has no action on glycogen or phosphorylase limit dextrin.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>The enzyme from intestinal mucosa is a single polypeptide chain that also catalyzes the reaction of EC 3.2.1.48.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>Differs from EC 3.2.1.33 in its preference for short-chain substrates and in its not requiring the 6-glucosylated residue to be at a branch point, i.e. linked at both C-1 and C-4.</rdfs:comment>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/291933"/>
