<rdfs:label>Endohydrolysis of (1->4)-alpha-D-glucosidic linkages in alpha-D-glucans containing both (1->3)- and (1->4)-bonds.</rdfs:label>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/5652425">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Citation"/>
<skos:exactMatch rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/pubmed/5652425"/>
<foaf:primaryTopicOf rdf:resource="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/5652425"/>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="3.2.1.62">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Enzyme"/>
<skos:prefLabel>glycosylceramidase</skos:prefLabel>
<skos:altLabel>cerebrosidase</skos:altLabel>
<skos:altLabel>glycosyl ceramide glycosylhydrolase</skos:altLabel>
<skos:altLabel>glycosyl-N-acylsphingosine glycohydrolase</skos:altLabel>
<skos:altLabel>lactase-phlorizin hydrolase</skos:altLabel>
<skos:altLabel>phloretin-glucosidase</skos:altLabel>
<skos:altLabel>phloridzin beta-glucosidase</skos:altLabel>
<skos:altLabel>phloridzin glucosidase</skos:altLabel>
<skos:altLabel>phlorizin hydrolase</skos:altLabel>
<activity rdf:resource="3.2.1.62#SIP7E7B8C3BE3D437AD"/>
<activity rdf:resource="3.2.1.62#SIPEC15549C3E25DAE0"/>
<activity rdf:resource="3.2.1.62#SIPBA20ADDBD92402CE"/>
<rdfs:comment>The enzyme, found in the intestinal mucosa, hydrolyzes beta-D-glucosyl and beta-D-galactosyl residues from a very broad range of substrates using a retaining mechanism.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>Characterized substrates include glucosyl- and galactosyl-ceramides, O(3)-, O(4') and O(7)-glucosylated flavonoids, and the 2'-O-glucosylated dihydrochalcone phlorizin.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>The enzyme includes two glycosyl hydrolase domains, both belonging to the GH1 family.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>While one domain is responsible for the activity described here, the other catalyzes the reaction of EC 3.2.1.108. cf. EC 3.2.1.45 and EC 3.2.1.46.</rdfs:comment>
