<skos:exactMatch rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/pubmed/23717145"/>
<foaf:primaryTopicOf rdf:resource="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23717145"/>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="3.2.1.192">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Enzyme"/>
<skos:prefLabel>ginsenoside Rb1 beta-glucosidase</skos:prefLabel>
<activity rdf:resource="3.2.1.192#SIP797C9475A5C5D93D"/>
<rdfs:comment>Ginsenosidases catalyze the hydrolysis of glycosyl moieties attached to the C-3, C-6 or C-20 position of ginsenosides.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>They are specific with respect to the nature of the glycosidic linkage, the position and the order in which the linkages are cleaved.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>Ginsenoside Rb1 beta-glucosidase specifically and sequentially hydrolyzes the 20-[beta-D-glucopyranosyl-(1->6)-beta-D glucopyranosyloxy] residues attached to position 20 by first hydrolyzing the (1->6)-glucosidic bond to generate ginsenoside Rd as an intermediate, followed by hydrolysis of the remaining 20-O-beta-D-glucosidic bond.</rdfs:comment>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/18256474"/>
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="3.2.1.-"/>
<skos:broaderTransitive rdf:resource="3.2.1.-"/>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="3.2.1.192#SIP797C9475A5C5D93D">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Catalytic_Activity"/>
<rdfs:label>(20S)-ginsenoside Rb1 + 2 H2O = (20S)-ginsenoside Rg3 + 2 D-glucose.</rdfs:label>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/18256474">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Citation"/>
<skos:exactMatch rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/pubmed/18256474"/>
<foaf:primaryTopicOf rdf:resource="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/18256474"/>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="3.2.1.193">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Enzyme"/>
<skos:prefLabel>ginsenosidase type I</skos:prefLabel>
<activity rdf:resource="3.2.1.193#SIP1E965EE7E1EF62E9"/>
<activity rdf:resource="3.2.1.193#SIPA5EFA16BBA849611"/>
<activity rdf:resource="3.2.1.193#SIP85BEA2E62FB139BF"/>
<rdfs:comment>The enzyme hydrolyzes the 3-O-beta-D-(1->2)-glucosidic bond, the 3-O-beta-D-glucopyranosyl bond and the 20-O-beta-D-(1->6)-glycosidic bond of protopanaxadiol-type ginsenosides.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>It usually leaves a single glucosyl residue attached at position 20 and one or no glucosyl residues at position 3.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>Starting with a ginsenoside that is glycosylated at both positions (e.g. ginsenoside Rb1, Rb2, Rb3, Rc or Rd), the most common products are ginsenoside F2 and ginsenoside C-K, with low amounts of ginsenoside Rh2.</rdfs:comment>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/17268094"/>
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="3.2.1.-"/>
<skos:broaderTransitive rdf:resource="3.2.1.-"/>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="3.2.1.193#SIP1E965EE7E1EF62E9">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Catalytic_Activity"/>
<rdfs:label>a protopanaxadiol-type ginsenoside with two glucosyl residues at position 3 + H2O = a protopanaxadiol-type ginsenoside with one glucosyl residue at position 3 + D-glucopyranose.</rdfs:label>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="3.2.1.193#SIPA5EFA16BBA849611">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Catalytic_Activity"/>
<rdfs:label>a protopanaxadiol-type ginsenoside with one glucosyl residue at position 3 + H2O = a protopanaxadiol-type ginsenoside with no glycosidic modifications at position 3 + D-glucopyranose.</rdfs:label>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="3.2.1.193#SIP85BEA2E62FB139BF">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Catalytic_Activity"/>
<rdfs:label>a protopanaxadiol-type ginsenoside with two glycosyl residues at position 20 + H2O = a protopanaxadiol-type ginsenoside with a single glucosyl residue at position 20 + a monosaccharide.</rdfs:label>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/17268094">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Citation"/>
<skos:exactMatch rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/pubmed/17268094"/>
<foaf:primaryTopicOf rdf:resource="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/17268094"/>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="3.2.1.194">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Enzyme"/>
<skos:prefLabel>ginsenosidase type IV</skos:prefLabel>
<activity rdf:resource="3.2.1.194#SIPF04E44EABA56AC06"/>
<activity rdf:resource="3.2.1.194#SIP6AC8D83C560DB9C1"/>
<activity rdf:resource="3.2.1.194#SIP5007B9450893B99D"/>
<activity rdf:resource="3.2.1.194#SIP4621E16A0806C9FF"/>
<rdfs:comment>Ginsenosidase type IV catalyzes the sequential hydrolysis of the 6-O-beta-D-(1->2)-glycosidic bond or the 6-O-alpha-D-(1->2)-glycosidic bond in protopanaxatriol-type ginsenosides with a disacchride attached to the C6 position, followed by the hydrolysis of the remaining 6-O-beta-D-glycosidic bond (e.g. ginsenoside Re -> ginsenoside Rg1 -> ginsenoside F1).</rdfs:comment>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/22031031"/>
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="3.2.1.-"/>
<skos:broaderTransitive rdf:resource="3.2.1.-"/>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="3.2.1.194#SIPF04E44EABA56AC06">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Catalytic_Activity"/>
<rdfs:label>a protopanaxatriol-type ginsenoside with two glycosyl residues at position 6 + 2 H2O = a protopanaxatriol-type ginsenoside with no glycosidic modification at position 6 + D-glucopyranose + a monosaccharide.</rdfs:label>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="3.2.1.194#SIP6AC8D83C560DB9C1">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Catalytic_Activity"/>
<rdfs:label>(20S)-ginsenoside Re + H2O = (20S)-ginsenoside Rg1 + L-rhamnopyranose.</rdfs:label>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="3.2.1.194#SIP5007B9450893B99D">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Catalytic_Activity"/>
<rdfs:label>(20S)-ginsenoside R1 + H2O = (20S)-ginsenoside Rg1 + D-xylose.</rdfs:label>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="3.2.1.194#SIP4621E16A0806C9FF">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Catalytic_Activity"/>
<rdfs:label>(20S)-ginsenoside Rg1 + H2O = (20S)-ginsenoside F1 + D-glucose.</rdfs:label>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/22031031">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Citation"/>
<skos:exactMatch rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/pubmed/22031031"/>
<foaf:primaryTopicOf rdf:resource="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/22031031"/>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="3.2.1.195">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Enzyme"/>
<skos:prefLabel>20-O-multi-glycoside ginsenosidase</skos:prefLabel>
<activity rdf:resource="3.2.1.195#SIP85BEA2E62FB139BF"/>
<activity rdf:resource="3.2.1.195#SIP7F7E2B5D15AF57DD"/>
<rdfs:comment>The 20-O-multi-glycoside ginsenosidase catalyzes the hydrolysis of the 20-O-alpha-(1->6)-glycosidic bond and the 20-O-beta-(1->6)-glycosidic bond of protopanaxadiol-type ginsenosides.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>The enzyme usually leaves a single glucosyl residue attached at position 20, although it can cleave the remaining glucosyl residue with a lower efficiency.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>Starting with a ginsenoside that is glycosylated at positions 3 and 20, such as ginsenosides Rb1, Rb2, Rb3 and Rc, the most common product is ginsenoside Rd, with a low amount of ginsenoside Rg3 also formed.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="3.2.1.-"/>
<skos:broaderTransitive rdf:resource="3.2.1.-"/>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="3.2.1.195#SIP85BEA2E62FB139BF">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Catalytic_Activity"/>
<rdfs:label>a protopanaxadiol-type ginsenoside with two glycosyl residues at position 20 + H2O = a protopanaxadiol-type ginsenoside with a single glucosyl residue at position 20 + a monosaccharide.</rdfs:label>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="3.2.1.195#SIP7F7E2B5D15AF57DD">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Catalytic_Activity"/>
<rdfs:label>(20S)-ginsenoside Rb1 + H2O = (20S)-ginsenoside Rd + D-glucose.</rdfs:label>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="3.2.1.196">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Enzyme"/>
<skos:prefLabel>limit dextrin alpha-1,6-maltotetraose-hydrolase</skos:prefLabel>
<skos:altLabel>glycogen debranching enzyme</skos:altLabel>
<activity rdf:resource="3.2.1.196#SIP9FE5C07B650F01A2"/>
<rdfs:comment>This bacterial enzyme catalyzes a reaction similar to EC 3.2.1.33.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>However, while EC 3.2.1.33 removes single glucose residues linked by 1,6-alpha-linkage, and thus requires the additional activity of EC 2.4.1.25 to act on limit dextrins formed by EC 2.4.1.1, this enzyme removes maltotriose and maltotetraose chains that are attached by 1,6-alpha-linkage to the limit dextrin main chain, generating a debranched limit dextrin without a need for another enzyme.</rdfs:comment>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/779849"/>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/15687211"/>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/20187119"/>
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="3.2.1.-"/>
<skos:broaderTransitive rdf:resource="3.2.1.-"/>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="3.2.1.196#SIP9FE5C07B650F01A2">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Catalytic_Activity"/>
<rdfs:label>Hydrolysis of (1->6)-alpha-D-glucosidic linkages to branches with degrees of polymerization of three or four glucose residues in limit dextrin.</rdfs:label>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/779849">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Citation"/>
<skos:exactMatch rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/pubmed/779849"/>
<foaf:primaryTopicOf rdf:resource="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/779849"/>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/15687211">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Citation"/>
<skos:exactMatch rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/pubmed/15687211"/>
<foaf:primaryTopicOf rdf:resource="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15687211"/>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/20187119">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Citation"/>
<skos:exactMatch rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/pubmed/20187119"/>
<foaf:primaryTopicOf rdf:resource="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20187119"/>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="3.2.1.197">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Enzyme"/>
<skos:prefLabel>beta-1,2-mannosidase</skos:prefLabel>
<activity rdf:resource="3.2.1.197#SIP7F95F0A970B233FD"/>
<rdfs:comment>The enzyme, characterized from multiple bacterial species, catalyzes the hydrolysis of terminal, non-reducing D-mannose residues from beta-1,2-mannotriose and beta-1,2-mannobiose.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>The mechanism involves anomeric inversion, resulting in the release of alpha-D-mannopyranose.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>Activity with beta-1,2-mannotriose or higher oligosaccharides is higher than that with beta-1,2-mannobiose.</rdfs:comment>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/26286752"/>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/26476324"/>
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="3.2.1.-"/>
<skos:broaderTransitive rdf:resource="3.2.1.-"/>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="3.2.1.197#SIP7F95F0A970B233FD">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Catalytic_Activity"/>
<rdfs:label>beta-D-Manp-(1->2)-beta-D-Manp-(1->2)-D-Manp + H2O = alpha-D-mannopyranose + beta-D-mannopyranosyl-(1->2)-D-mannopyranose.</rdfs:label>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/26286752">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Citation"/>
<skos:exactMatch rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/pubmed/26286752"/>
<foaf:primaryTopicOf rdf:resource="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26286752"/>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/26476324">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Citation"/>
<skos:exactMatch rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/pubmed/26476324"/>
<foaf:primaryTopicOf rdf:resource="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26476324"/>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="3.2.1.198">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Enzyme"/>
<skos:prefLabel>alpha-mannan endo-1,2-alpha-mannanase</skos:prefLabel>
<activity rdf:resource="3.2.1.198#SIP24924B3BB528716A"/>
<rdfs:comment>The enzyme, characterized from the gut bacteria Bacteroides thetaiotaomicron and Bacteroides xylanisolvens, can also catalyze the reaction of EC 3.2.1.130.</rdfs:comment>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/25487964"/>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/25567280"/>
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="3.2.1.-"/>
<skos:broaderTransitive rdf:resource="3.2.1.-"/>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="3.2.1.198#SIP24924B3BB528716A">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Catalytic_Activity"/>
<rdfs:label>Hydrolysis of the terminal alpha-D-mannosyl-(1->3)-alpha-D-mannose disaccharide from alpha-D-mannosyl-(1->3)-alpha-D-mannosyl-(1->2)-alpha-D-mannosyl-(1->2)-alpha-D-mannosyl side chains in fungal cell wall alpha-mannans.</rdfs:label>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/25487964">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Citation"/>
<skos:exactMatch rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/pubmed/25487964"/>
<foaf:primaryTopicOf rdf:resource="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/25487964"/>
</rdf:Description>
