<rdf:Description rdf:about="3.2.1.208">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Enzyme"/>
<skos:prefLabel>glucosylglycerate hydrolase</skos:prefLabel>
<activity rdf:resource="3.2.1.208#SIPEF4F52AD2C57911D"/>
<rdfs:comment>The enzyme has been isolated from non-tuberculous mycobacteria (e.g. Mycobacterium hassiacum), which accumulate 2-O-(alpha-D-glucopyranosyl)-D-glycerate during growth under nitrogen deprivation.</rdfs:comment>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/25341489"/>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/28876234"/>
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="3.2.1.-"/>
<skos:broaderTransitive rdf:resource="3.2.1.-"/>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="3.2.1.208#SIPEF4F52AD2C57911D">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Catalytic_Activity"/>
<rdfs:label>(2R)-2-O-(alpha-D-glucopyranosyl)-glycerate + H2O = (R)-glycerate + D-glucose.</rdfs:label>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/25341489">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Citation"/>
<skos:exactMatch rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/pubmed/25341489"/>
<foaf:primaryTopicOf rdf:resource="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/25341489"/>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/28876234">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Citation"/>
<skos:exactMatch rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/pubmed/28876234"/>
<foaf:primaryTopicOf rdf:resource="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/28876234"/>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="3.2.1.209">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Enzyme"/>
<skos:prefLabel>endoplasmic reticulum Man9GlcNAc2 1,2-alpha-mannosidase</skos:prefLabel>
<activity rdf:resource="3.2.1.209#SIP68BB3EBF1A83FBB8"/>
<rdfs:comment>The enzyme, located in the endoplasmic reticulum, primarily trims a single alpha-1,2-linked mannose residue from Man9GlcNAc2 to produce Man8GlcNAc2 isomer 8A1,2,3B1,3 (the names of the isomers listed here are based on a nomenclature system proposed by Prien et al (pmid:19181540)).</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>The removal of the single mannosyl residue occurs in all eukaryotes as part of the processing of N-glycosylated proteins, and is absolutely essential for further elongation of the outer chain of properly-folded N-glycosylated proteins in yeast.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>In addition, the enzyme is involved in glycoprotein quality control at the ER quality control compartment (ERQC), helping to target misfolded glycoproteins for degradation.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>When present at very high concentrations in the ERQC, the enzyme can trim the carbohydrate chain further to Man(5-6)GlcNAc2.</rdfs:comment>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/1822240"/>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/3049586"/>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/10409699"/>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/12090241"/>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/18003979"/>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/19181540"/>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/20023195"/>
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="3.2.1.-"/>
<skos:broaderTransitive rdf:resource="3.2.1.-"/>
</rdf:Description>
