<skos:prefLabel>microsomal epoxide hydrolase</skos:prefLabel>
<skos:altLabel>arene-oxide hydratase</skos:altLabel>
<skos:altLabel>aryl epoxide hydrase</skos:altLabel>
<skos:altLabel>benzo[a]pyrene-4,5-oxide hydratase</skos:altLabel>
<skos:altLabel>cis-epoxide hydrolase</skos:altLabel>
<skos:altLabel>epoxide hydrase</skos:altLabel>
<skos:altLabel>epoxide hydratase</skos:altLabel>
<skos:altLabel>mEH</skos:altLabel>
<skos:altLabel>microsomal epoxide hydrase</skos:altLabel>
<skos:altLabel>microsomal epoxide hydratase</skos:altLabel>
<skos:altLabel>microsomal oxirane/oxetane hydrolase</skos:altLabel>
<activity rdf:resource="3.3.2.9#SIPB1E2CED4D0334987"/>
<activity rdf:resource="3.3.2.9#SIPFDB901FABFC40FAD"/>
<activity rdf:resource="3.3.2.9#SIPF961E0089025C526"/>
<rdfs:comment>This is a key hepatic enzyme that catalyzes the hydrolytic ring opening of oxiranes (epoxides) and oxetanes to give the corresponding diols.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>The enzyme is involved in the metabolism of numerous substrates including the stereoselective hydrolytic ring opening of 7-oxabicyclo[4.1.0]hepta-2,4-dienes (arene oxides) to the corresponding trans-dihydrodiols.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>The reaction proceeds via a triad mechanism and involves the formation of an hydroxyalkyl-enzyme intermediate.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>Five epoxide-hydrolase enzymes have been identified in vertebrates to date: EC 3.3.2.6, EC 3.3.2.7, EC 3.3.2.9, EC 3.3.2.10 and EC 3.3.2.11.</rdfs:comment>
