<skos:prefLabel>proteasome endopeptidase complex</skos:prefLabel>
<skos:altLabel>ingensin</skos:altLabel>
<skos:altLabel>Lens neutral proteinase</skos:altLabel>
<skos:altLabel>macropain</skos:altLabel>
<skos:altLabel>multicatalytic endopeptidase complex</skos:altLabel>
<skos:altLabel>multicatalytic proteinase (complex)</skos:altLabel>
<skos:altLabel>prosome</skos:altLabel>
<skos:altLabel>proteasome</skos:altLabel>
<activity rdf:resource="3.4.25.1#SIPC4F9DC763185FEFF"/>
<rdfs:comment>A 20-S protein composed of 28 subunits arranged in four rings of seven.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>The outer rings are composed of alpha subunits, but the beta subunits forming the inner rings are responsible for peptidase activity.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>In eukaryotic organisms there are up to seven different types of beta subunits, three of which may carry the N-terminal threonine residues that are the nucleophiles in catalysis, and show different specificities.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>The molecule is barrel-shaped, and the active sites are on the inner surfaces.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>Terminal apertures restrict access of substrates to the active sites.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>Inhibited by mercurial reagents and some inhibitors of serine endopeptidases.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>Belongs to peptidase family T1.</rdfs:comment>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/7725107"/>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/8811196"/>
