<rdf:Description rdf:about="4.2.2.21">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Enzyme"/>
<replaces rdf:resource="4.2.2.4"/>
<replaces rdf:resource="4.2.99.6"/>
<skos:prefLabel>chondroitin-sulfate-ABC exolyase</skos:prefLabel>
<skos:altLabel>chondroitin ABC eliminase</skos:altLabel>
<skos:altLabel>chondroitin ABC lyase</skos:altLabel>
<skos:altLabel>chondroitinase</skos:altLabel>
<skos:altLabel>chondroitinase ABC</skos:altLabel>
<skos:altLabel>chondroitin sulfate ABC exoeliminase</skos:altLabel>
<skos:altLabel>chondroitin sulfate ABC exolyase</skos:altLabel>
<skos:altLabel>chondroitin sulfate ABC lyase</skos:altLabel>
<skos:altLabel>ChS ABC lyase</skos:altLabel>
<skos:altLabel>ChS ABC lyase II</skos:altLabel>
<activity rdf:resource="4.2.2.21#SIPE9FA125890DC3C36"/>
<rdfs:comment>Degrades a variety of glycosaminoglycans of the chondroitin-sulfate- and dermatan-sulfate type.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>Chondroitin sulfate, chondroitin-sulfate proteoglycan and dermatan sulfate are the best substrates but the enzyme can also act on hyaluronan at a much lower rate.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>The related enzyme EC 4.2.2.20 has the same substrate specificity but produces a mixture of oligosaccharides of different sizes that are ultimately degraded to tetra- and disaccharides.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>Both enzymes act by the removal of a relatively acidic C-5 proton of the uronic acid followed by the elimination of a 4-linked hexosamine, resulting in the formation of an unsaturated C4-C5 bond on the hexuronic acid moiety of the products.</rdfs:comment>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/4231029"/>
