<rdf:Description rdf:about="4.3.1.16">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Enzyme"/>
<skos:prefLabel>threo-3-hydroxy-L-aspartate ammonia-lyase</skos:prefLabel>
<skos:altLabel>L-threo-3-hydroxyaspartate dehydratase</skos:altLabel>
<skos:altLabel>threo-3-hydroxyaspartate dehydratase</skos:altLabel>
<activity rdf:resource="4.3.1.16#SIPE3330F4855B58C94"/>
<rdfs:comment>The enzyme, purified from the bacterium Pseudomonas sp. T62, is highly specific, and does not accept any other stereoisomer of 3-hydroxyaspartate.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>Different from EC 4.3.1.20 and EC 4.3.1.27.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>Requires a divalent cation such as Mn(2+), Mg(2+), or Ca(2+).</rdfs:comment>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/10481099"/>
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="4.3.1.-"/>
<skos:broaderTransitive rdf:resource="4.3.1.-"/>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="4.3.1.16#SIPE3330F4855B58C94">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Catalytic_Activity"/>
<rdfs:label>(3S)-3-hydroxy-L-aspartate = NH4(+) + oxaloacetate.</rdfs:label>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/10481099">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Citation"/>
<skos:exactMatch rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/pubmed/10481099"/>
<foaf:primaryTopicOf rdf:resource="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10481099"/>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="4.3.1.17">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Enzyme"/>
<replaces rdf:resource="4.2.1.13"/>
<skos:prefLabel>L-serine ammonia-lyase</skos:prefLabel>
<skos:altLabel>L-hydroxyaminoacid dehydratase</skos:altLabel>
<skos:altLabel>L-serine deaminase</skos:altLabel>
<skos:altLabel>L-serine dehydratase</skos:altLabel>
<skos:altLabel>L-serine hydro-lyase (deaminating)</skos:altLabel>
<skos:altLabel>serine deaminase</skos:altLabel>
<activity rdf:resource="4.3.1.17#SIP61B17FF44964E6E0"/>
<rdfs:comment>The reaction catalyzed by both types of enzymes involves the initial elimination of water to form an enamine intermediate (hence the enzyme's original classification as EC 4.2.1.13) followed by tautomerization to an imine form and hydrolysis of the C-N bond.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>The latter reaction, which can occur spontaneously, is also be catalyzed by EC 3.5.99.10.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>This reaction is also carried out by EC 4.3.1.19 from a number of sources.</rdfs:comment>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/4750769"/>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/7042346"/>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/8236444"/>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/14596599"/>
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="4.3.1.-"/>
<skos:broaderTransitive rdf:resource="4.3.1.-"/>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="4.3.1.17#SIP61B17FF44964E6E0">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Catalytic_Activity"/>
<rdfs:label>L-serine = NH4(+) + pyruvate.</rdfs:label>
</rdf:Description>
