<rdf:Description rdf:about="4.4.1.13">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Enzyme"/>
<replaces rdf:resource="4.4.1.6"/>
<replaces rdf:resource="4.4.1.8"/>
<skos:prefLabel>cysteine-S-conjugate beta-lyase</skos:prefLabel>
<skos:altLabel>alkylcysteine lyase</skos:altLabel>
<skos:altLabel>alkyl cysteine lyase</skos:altLabel>
<skos:altLabel>cystathionine beta-lyase</skos:altLabel>
<skos:altLabel>cystine lyase</skos:altLabel>
<skos:altLabel>L-cysteine-S-conjugate thiol-lyase (deaminating)</skos:altLabel>
<skos:altLabel>S-alkylcysteinase</skos:altLabel>
<skos:altLabel>S-alkylcysteine lyase</skos:altLabel>
<skos:altLabel>S-alkyl-L-cysteine lyase</skos:altLabel>
<skos:altLabel>S-alkyl-L-cysteine sulfoxide lyase</skos:altLabel>
<activity rdf:resource="4.4.1.13#SIPE525A955560BD899"/>
<rdfs:comment>A pyridoxal 5'-phosphate protein.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>The enzyme is promiscuous regarding the moiety conjugated to L-cysteine, and can accept both aliphatic and aromatic substitutions.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>The enzyme cleaves a carbon-sulfur bond, releasing a thiol and an unstable enamine product that tautomerizes to an imine form, which undergoes a hydrolytic deamination to form pyruvate and ammonia.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>While bacteria and plants have dedicated enzymes, all of the animal enzymes discovered thus far are bifunctional, most of which also act as aminotransferases.</rdfs:comment>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/721818"/>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/3782077"/>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/4008484"/>
