<rdf:Description rdf:about="5.3.1.25">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Enzyme"/>
<skos:prefLabel>L-fucose isomerase</skos:prefLabel>
<activity rdf:resource="5.3.1.25#SIP518F5C08083BF036"/>
<rdfs:comment>The enzyme binds the closed form of the sugar and catalyzes ring opening to generate a form of open-chain conformation that facilitates the isomerization reaction, which proceeds via an ene-diol mechanism.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>The enzyme from Escherichia coli can also convert D-arabinose to D-ribulose.</rdfs:comment>
<rdfs:comment>The enzyme from the thermophilic bacterium Caldicellulosiruptor saccharolyticus also converts D-altrose to D-psicose and L-galactose to L-tagatose.</rdfs:comment>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/2664711"/>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/9367760"/>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/13319278"/>
<citation rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/citations/19856146"/>
<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="5.3.1.-"/>
<skos:broaderTransitive rdf:resource="5.3.1.-"/>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="5.3.1.25#SIP518F5C08083BF036">
<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.uniprot.org/core/Catalytic_Activity"/>
<rdfs:label>L-fucose = L-fuculose.</rdfs:label>
</rdf:Description>
